course: dictionary
lesson: 13777
title: Intimidation and fear
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Intimidation means frightening someone, often with threats, so that they do what you want or are too scared to act. Its root is the word intimidate, which means to make someone afraid. When you meet it, look for the clues around it: threats, fear, and someone being made to do something.
explain: «The older boy used intimidation to make the others hand over their lunch money.» The clues are the lunch money and the words made to hand over. The others did not give their money freely. They gave it because they were scared, so intimidation must mean frightening someone to get what you want.
explain: Intimidation is often used in school safety and anti-bullying lessons, because it is a form of bullying. It can happen without any hitting at all. A threat, a hard stare or a whispered warning can be enough to frighten someone into silence.
explain: In the news you may read about «voter intimidation». This means scaring people with threats so they do not vote freely. When you see the word, ask yourself: is someone using fear as a weapon?
fact: Intimidation means frightening someone, often with threats.
fact: People use intimidation to make others do what they want, or to stop them from speaking up.
fact: Intimidation is a form of bullying, and it can happen with words, looks or gestures, not just with hitting.
fact: In the news, «voter intimidation» means scaring people so they do not vote freely.
fact: A good check is to swap in the phrase «scaring someone with threats» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The older boy used intimidation to make the others hand over their lunch money.» What does intimidation mean? | Sharing your lunch with everyone | *Frightening someone with threats so they do what you want | Telling a joke to cheer people up | Making friends with someone new
exercise: «Threats and hard stares are forms of intimidation.» What does the sentence say threats and hard stares are? | Kind gifts | Ways to say sorry | Signs of a happy day | *Forms of intimidation
exercise: «The witness was too scared to speak after the threats from the man outside.» Why was the witness too scared to speak? | Because the room was too hot | Because they were tired | *Because someone frightened them with threats | Because they had forgotten the story
test: Which of these is an example of intimidation? | *Telling someone, "Give me your sweets or else" | Waving hello to a friend | Asking politely to borrow a pencil | Helping a classmate with maths
test: In the news, «voter intimidation» means | Counting the votes very fast | Giving everyone a free ride to vote | *Scaring people with threats so they do not vote freely | Helping people find their polling station
exam: Which sentence shows intimidation? | The girl read a book in the library | The girl shared her crisps at break | The girl gave her friend a big hug | *The girl said, "If you tell, you will be sorry," so her friend stayed quiet
